      "narration": "Here is the picture I want you to keep, because backpropagation is the one piece of deep learning that people find genuinely frightening, and it stops being frightening the moment it is a kitchen. A dish goes out of the Baratie and comes straight back: too salty. Now, Zeff has a choice about how to respond. The stupid response is to shout at everyone, which is the equivalent of nudging every weight at random. The slow response is to run an experiment per station — remake the dish nine times, changing one station's work each time, and see which change fixes it. That is finite differences, and in a busy kitchen it is not available. What Zeff actually does is walk the complaint backward along the line that produced the dish. The plating station gets the message first, because it was last. And plating asks itself exactly one question: how much of “too salty” is my doing? Almost none — plating arranged things on a plate, it did not season anything. So plating keeps a tiny share of the blame and passes nearly all of it back to the station before it. The sauce station receives what is left, and asks itself the same question. Its answer is: quite a lot, actually, that is my reduction. So it keeps a large share — that is its gradient, and it tells the saucier exactly how much less salt to use next time — and it passes the remainder back to prep, who seasoned the stock. Prep does the same. Now notice the two properties that make this work, because they are the two properties of backpropagation. First, no station has to understand the whole kitchen. The saucier does not need to know how plating works or what the customer said, only how much saltier the dish gets if the sauce gets a pinch more salt. Each station multiplies the blame it was handed by its own local effect. That is the chain rule wearing an apron. Second, and this is the saving, it is one walk back. One dish came back, one complaint travelled down the line, and every station on that line now knows exactly how much to change what it does. Not one experiment per station: one pass. That is the difference between a kitchen that improves and a kitchen that shouts, and it is exactly the difference between a network you can train and a network you cannot.",
